# central-config environment variables.
#
# Copy to .env for local development. .env is gitignored and must stay that way.
# In production inject these through Kubernetes secrets/configmaps or your
# secret store — do not ship a populated .env in an image.
#
# Every value below is a dummy. Defaults shown in [brackets] are what the
# process uses when the variable is unset.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Storage — the source of truth
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# "postgres" for production, "sqlite" for the local test stack
# (internal/database/sqlite.go). [postgres]
DB_DRIVER=sqlite

# PostgreSQL DSN when DB_DRIVER=postgres. Either shape libpq accepts works —
# the driver is pgx through database/sql:
#   postgres://USER:PASSWORD@host:5432/DATABASE?sslmode=require
#   host=… port=5432 user=… password=… dbname=… sslmode=require
# SQLite file path when DB_DRIVER=sqlite. []
CONN_STRING=file:./central-config.db?_pragma=busy_timeout(5000)

# Connection pool bounds, applied to PostgreSQL only — the SQLite test stack
# runs on a single connection so its writers stay serialized. Left unbounded, a
# slow query plus two replicas plus the reconciler can open connections until
# the server hits max_connections; and because every Postgres connection is a
# backend process, the cost of approaching that limit lands on every other
# consumer of the instance before the refusals even start.

# Concurrent connections this process may hold. Per replica, so multiply by
# your replica count before comparing it against the server's
# max_connections. [20]
DB_MAX_OPEN_CONNS=20

# Idle connections kept open for reuse. [5]
DB_MAX_IDLE_CONNS=5

# How long a connection may live before it is retired. Without a lifetime a
# connection survives a failover or a pgbouncer restart as a half-dead socket
# that only fails when it is next used. Go duration syntax. [30m]
DB_CONN_MAX_LIFETIME=30m

# How long an idle connection is kept before it is closed. [5m]
DB_CONN_MAX_IDLE_TIME=5m

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Tests — the PostgreSQL integration suite
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Read by internal/pgintegration, and by nothing the service itself runs.
# Unset, that suite skips every test, which is what keeps `go test ./...` green
# on a machine with no database. Set, it applies migrations/*.sql into a schema
# of its own per test and exercises the real repositories against them — the
# only coverage the production storage path has.
#
# It is deliberately not CONN_STRING. The suite creates and drops schemas, and
# pointing it at whatever a developer happened to export for `make run` is not
# a mistake worth leaving possible. Give it a throwaway database:
# `make test-postgres` starts one, runs the suite against it and removes it.
#   postgres://USER:PASSWORD@host:5432/DATABASE?sslmode=disable
# []
TEST_POSTGRES_DSN=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HTTP server
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Listen address for the admin API. [:8080]
PORT=:8080

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Messaging / JetStream — the distribution plane
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Set true to write through to KV on every admin write and run the reconciler.
# With this false the service is a database-backed CRUD API and nothing is
# distributed. [false]
PUBLISH_ENABLED=true

# NATS server or cluster address. []
NATS_URL=nats://localhost:4222

# Optional path to a NATS .creds file (NKEY/JWT). Empty means no NATS auth,
# which is dev-only. []
NATS_CREDS=

# KV bucket replica count: 1 for single-node dev, 3 for a production cluster.
# Only applied when the bucket is created. [1]
NATS_REPLICAS=1

# How often the reconciler resyncs the database to KV and heals drift. Go
# duration syntax. [5m]
RECONCILE_INTERVAL=5m

# The share of a KV bucket one reconcile cycle may delete. A sweep that wants
# to remove more than this is refused and logged instead: a database that came
# back empty, or a query that failed open, would otherwise have the reconciler
# purge the configuration of every consuming service. Must be in (0,1]. [0.2]
RECONCILE_PRUNE_MAX_FRACTION=0.2

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Security
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Every route except /health, /livez and /metrics needs a token — the reads as
# well as the writes. A token's scope narrows both: it may only write to the
# environments it names, and it only sees those environments' rows in the
# listings, in /inventory and in the audit trail.

# Named tokens, comma separated: NAME:ENV_SCOPE:SECRET
#   ENV_SCOPE is "*" for all environments, or environment ids joined by "|".
# The name is recorded as the actor on every audit row, and is limited to
# letters, digits, dot, dash and underscore. A SECRET may contain colons but
# NOT a comma: entries are split on commas first, so a comma inside a secret
# silently starts an entry — and possibly a token — of its own.
# Example (dummy secrets — generate your own):
#   ADMIN_TOKENS=alice:*:CHANGE_ME_ADMIN,ci-dev:1|2:CHANGE_ME_CI
ADMIN_TOKENS=

# The single shared token, recorded as the "shared" full-scope actor. It is not
# comma separated, so a comma in this one is harmless.
# If both this and ADMIN_TOKENS are empty, auth is
# DISABLED and the service logs a warning at startup — dev only. []
ADMIN_TOKEN=change-me-local-dev-token

# Writes allowed per caller per minute; <= 0 disables the limiter. [120]
WRITE_RATE_LIMIT_PER_MINUTE=120

# When both are set the admin API serves HTTPS, with a TLS 1.3 floor and an
# HSTS header on every response. Otherwise plain HTTP with a startup warning —
# acceptable only behind a TLS-terminating ingress, which is then where HSTS
# belongs too. []
TLS_CERT_FILE=
TLS_KEY_FILE=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Observability
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# debug | info | warn | error [info]
LOG_LEVEL=info

# "json" emits ECS documents for Elasticsearch; "text" is the readable
# local-development shape. [json]
LOG_FORMAT=text

# Stamped onto every log line. [central-config] / [dev]
SERVICE_NAME=central-config
SERVICE_VERSION=dev

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Test console (cmd/testconsole) — local stack only, not the service
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The console plays a consuming microservice: it holds a live configclient
# cache and serves a browser UI showing that cache update in real time.

# Environment id the console's consumer watches. [1]
ENVIRONMENT_ID=1

# Admin API the console proxies its writes to. [http://localhost:8080]
CENTRAL_CONFIG_URL=http://localhost:8080

# Run a JetStream server inside the console process, so the stack works without
# Docker. The compose stack leaves this off and uses the nats container. [false]
NATS_EMBEDDED=false

# Directory holding the built React bundle (webui/ builds to web/). [web]
WEB_DIR=web

# The console also reads NATS_URL, ADMIN_TOKEN and PORT. In the compose stack
# it listens on :8090; set PORT accordingly when running it alongside the
# service on one machine.
